#: ../../source/foundation-log.rst:3121 b6d21adcba36400e8abf52e62a88e69f
#, python-format
msgid ""
"**A constant that had never been executed was wrong by 18 %, and it was "
"the one the plan was about to trust.** `oracle_ornl.py` carried "
"`LOCKED_ROTOR = [(75, 140), (150, 260), (250, 400)]`, transcribed from "
"ORNL/TM-2006/423's *summary* Table 6.3, and nothing in the repository "
"ever read it. The primary data is Table 3.2 (p.19): 7 currents x 23 shaft"
" angles, whose column peaks are **74.0 / 117.7 / 158.0 / 199.0 / 229.0 / "
"286.6 / 339.0 N.m** at 50..250 A. Every summary value is **1.14 to "
"1.19x** the primary peak at the same current (1.189 / 1.135 / 1.180), "
"i.e. a constant normalisation difference rather than a transcription slip"
" -- and using it as \"the measurement\" would have reported an 18 % error"
" in a parameter set that is within 3 %."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/foundation-log.rst:3129 84a4aa3038234ae7b141e9a48b6080c4
msgid ""
"Surfaced by finally using it, as the cheap pre-check for B-2's charter. "
"The 1.80x gap between a 75 A point and an 82 N.m prediction was too large"
" to be physics, so the table was re-read from the PDF with `-layout`: "
"that also settled the unit -- the currents are **per-phase rms** (peak = "
"sqrt(2) I), proved by the 50 A point landing at **+0.7 %** and 125 A at "
"**+0.2 %** under that convention, against **-42 %** if the column value "
"were taken as a peak."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/foundation-log.rst:3134 ccafa455ed384f05aca4242969c1b385
msgid ""
"No kernel claim moves: this is `IPMSM_A`, which A-91 already says is not "
"this library's machine. What changes is the tool and the statement of "
"what the data can do. `--torque` now carries Table 3.2, a transcription "
"self-check (peaks monotone, mean below peak, the 6.3/3.2 ratio constant),"
" and a **sensitivity scan printed on every run**: these seven points pin "
"`psi_f` to **[0.93, 1.02]x** the ORNL value -- which rejects the FEMM "
"route (0.18826 Wb, **1.089x**, out of band at 150 A) on a measurement "
"that does not share its assumptions -- and pin `Lq - Ld` to essentially "
"**nothing** (`k` in [0, 1.1] passes, and `k = 0` passes, because the "
"reluctance term is a small share of the torque at these currents). The "
"first version of this check claimed to validate `Lq - Ld`; its own "
"sensitivity scan is what forced that sentence to be withdrawn, and it is "
"printed every run so the same claim cannot creep back."
msgstr ""

